      "explain": "Cognitive load theory (Sweller, van Merriënboer and Paas 2019) separates intrinsic load, which comes from how many elements interact in the material itself, from extraneous load, which comes from how the material is presented. The history, the shifting notation and the split between formula and numbers are all presentation problems, so A removes extraneous load without touching the content. Option B reduces intrinsic load by teaching less, which is a different trade. Option C makes the overload worse. Option D is good practice in general (lesson 3) but does nothing about the exhaustion caused by the presentation."
