      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "A partly forgotten memory has to be rebuilt on review, and rebuilding is retrieval practice, the strongest tool there is. A fresh memory is merely recognised, and recognition does not exercise the route. That is the mechanism behind the spacing effect, and why the best gap is long enough to allow some forgetting. Option C treats forgetting as failure. Option B would have raised fluency in session one and changed little at a week. Option D invents a complexity rule the evidence does not support; Cepeda's fractions apply across verbal material generally."
